columns --all: swept 2 table(s), 1 carrying a finding.
columns is a CANDIDATE lens. READS are the union of two arms — language-service `<table>.<col>` query references PLUS a row-shape scan for `<row>.<col>` reads (needed because `$inferSelect` is a mapped type: a read through a declared row alias is INVISIBLE to reference resolution). The row-shape arm is deliberately OVER-inclusive, so a read count can be generous — which is the safe direction. WRITES are STRUCTURAL for the same mapped-type reason, so a table with a whole-row/spread writer marks every column `write?`, never "unwritten". A `raw?` annotation means the column's SQL name appears in some raw `sql` template — NOT attributable to a table in v1, so read those before calling it rot. A `created_at`/`updated_at` with a schema DEFAULT that nothing reads back is classed `provenance`, counted in the table above and NEVER listed as a hit — an audit stamp is not the RV-11 class. Keep one deliberately with `// @column-ok: <reason>` on the column property.
